 The problem requires finding the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$ that projects any vector $\mathbf{v}$ onto the vector $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}$. The projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$ onto a vector $\mathbf{a}$ is given by: \[
\mathbf{P} = \frac{\mathbf{a} \mathbf{a}^T}{\mathbf{a}^T \mathbf{a}}
\] First, we compute $\mathbf{a} \mathbf{a}^T$: \[
\mathbf{a} \mathbf{a}^T = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -2 & -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -4 & -2 \\ -4 & 4 & 2 \\ -2 & 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\] Next, we compute $\mathbf{a}^T \mathbf{a}$: \[
\mathbf{a}^T \mathbf{a} = 2^2 + (-2)^2 + (-1)^2 = 4 + 4 + 1 = 9
\] Therefore, the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$ is: \[
\mathbf{P} = \frac{1}{9} \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -4 & -2 \\ -4 & 4 & 2 \\ -2 & 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{4}{9} & -\frac{4}{9} & -\frac{2}{9} \\ -\frac{4}{9} & \frac{4}{9} & \frac{2}{9} \\ -\frac{2}{9} & \frac{2}{9} & \frac{1}{9} \end{pmatrix}
\] </think> <answer> $\begin{pmatrix} \frac{4}{9} & -\frac{4}{9} & -\frac{2}{9} \\ -\frac{4}{9} & \frac{4}{9} & \frac{2}{9} \\ -\frac{2}{9} & \frac{2}{9} & \frac{1}{9} \end{pmatrix} $ </answer>

